fig, I'll try it with the standard=0A> > linux kernel.=0A> =0A> linux-yocto=
-3.10 is pretty much the standard kernel, unless you want to use=0A> the ex=
tra features I've merged, it is vanilla.=0A> =0A> You are going overboard w=
ith the definitions. What the build really needs, it'll=0A> generate on it'=
s own. If you want to make an official/controlled BSP=0A> ... only then=0A>=
 do you actually need .scc files, etc.=0A> =0A> If you add compatbility wit=
h the linux-yocto recipe, put a defconfig in the=0A> SRC_URI and you are do=
ne.=0A> =0A> Bruce=0A=0AOk here is the defconfig I used for a15. I disabled=
 SMP because it was=0Aannoying me more than helping. I did have to use the =
dtb generated as=0Awell, otherwise it wouldn't work.=0A=0AThis is the typic=
al qemu command line I used:=0A=0Aqemu-system-arm -cpu cortex-a15 -M vexpre=
ss-a15 -kernel=0Aimages/zImage-qemuarmv7a.bin -append"console=3DttyAMA0 mem=
=3D512M=0Aroot=3D/dev/mmcblk0 rw" -m 512 -dtb=0Atmp/deploy/images/zImage-ve=
xpress-v2p-ca15_a7.dtb -sd=0Atmp/deploy/images/core-image-minimal-qemuarmv7=
a.ext3 -net nic -net=0Auser,hostfwd=3Dtcp::2400-:22 -nographic=0A=0AI also =
made my own machine configuration so of course the name of the=0Arootfilesy=
stem file will be different. As Bruce said, I'm sure this=0Acould be plumbe=
d in to the yocto infrastructure so that runqemu and the=0Avarious helpers =
could be used, but I was trying to get an sdk out to=0Asomeone fairly quick=
ly.=0A=0A> >=0A> > I'm doing this to get the process of using Yocto to buil=
d a representative=0A> > QEMU emulator, and then using LaunchPad and Soyuz =
locally to=0A> > build Ubuntu on a virtual machine, rather than just re-use=
 the official=0A> > Ubuntu binary packages. This way, I have more control o=
